<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri Bergson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Henri-Louis Bergson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henri Bergson was a French philosopher, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, who argued that immediate experience and intuition matter more than abstract rationalism and science for understanding reality. His four principal works were Time and Free Will (1889), Matter and Memory (1896), Creative Evolution (1907) and The Two Sources of Morality and Religion (1932). He held the chair of Greek and Roman philosophy and then that of modern philosophy at the Collège de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where the public attended his open courses in large numbers. He received the 1927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature, and his popularity caused controversy in France, where his views were seen as opposed to the Republic's secular and scientific attitude.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-bergson.jpg" alt="Henri Bergson" width="330" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Manuel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_Bergson_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bergson was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 18 October 1859. His father, Michał Bergson, was a composer and pianist of Polish-Jewish background, and his mother, Katherine Levison, the daughter of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> doctor, was of English-Jewish and Irish-Jewish background. The family lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for a few years after his birth, so he learned English from his mother, and before he was nine his parents settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. At the Lycée Fontanes he won a prize in 1877 for solving a mathematical problem, and after some hesitation chose the humanities over the sciences, to his teachers' dismay. He entered the École Normale Supérieure at nineteen and took his agrégation in philosophy in 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bergson taught at the lycée in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont-Ferrand</a>. In 1900 the Collège de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> appointed him to the chair of Greek and Roman philosophy, and from 1904 to 1920 he held the chair of modern philosophy in succession to Gabriel Tarde. In 1908 he met William James in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and James, his senior by 17 years, brought his work to Anglo-American readers. In 1913 he lectured to large audiences in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He presided from 1920 to 1925 over the International Committee on Intellectual Cooperation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, whose members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-curie">Marie Curie</a>. In 1922 he published Duration and Simultaneity, a response to Einstein's theory, whose merits remain debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Bergson rejected the mechanistic view of causality and held that free will unfolds in an autonomous and unpredictable way. Against Kant, he argued that philosophers had confused time with its spatial representation; real duration is unextended yet heterogeneous, so determinism is impossible. Duration can be grasped only through intuition, not immobile concepts. In Creative Evolution (1907) he introduced the élan vital, a vital impetus meant to explain evolution and human creativity; he nonetheless criticised vitalism in the same book. His essay Laughter treats the comic as a caricature of the mechanical in human habit. He received the 1927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature and the Grand-Croix of the Legion of Honour in 1930. He died on 4 January 1941.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henri Bergson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sigmund Freud placed the unconscious at the centre of mental life, and <b>Henri Bergson</b>’s idea of subjective time deeply influenced later novelists.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
